From openbsd-alpha  Wed Mar 03 05:07:19 2004
From: rcooley <rcooley () spamcop ! net>
Date: Wed, 03 Mar 2004 05:07:19 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: Re: Alphaserver DS20 - Error booting OpenBSD - Hangs on "Root device:"
Message-Id: <20040302210719.7c5ddea3.rcooley () spamcop ! net>
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=107829056900509

On Wed, 3 Mar 2004 00:50:30 +0100
"Mads Toustrup" <madst@ikasths.dk> wrote:

> but suddenly the bootprocess stops at "root device:"
> 
> Do you know the reason why my system stops at this point? 
 
I had the same problem when I tried to boot from a hard drive that was
the slave on the IDE channel...  Swaping it to become the master solved
my problem.

Someone else asked me for help with the same problem, but using SCSI
disks...  The solution he had was to change a line in the kernel config
from this:

config         bsd     swap generic

...to this:

config         bsd     root on sd0a swap on sd0b

So, if you are able to boot your system once (I would suggest using
a serial console, that will probably give you keyboard control)... You
just need to change that line to match your boot/swap devices, and then
compile and use that new kernel.

